  "outline": [
    "1. Carbohydrate Basics: synthesized via photosynthesis; 1g=4kcal; USDA 45-65% daily calories; classification by monomer count (mono/di/oligo/poly) linked via glycosidic bonds",
    "2. Monosaccharide Structure & Naming: composed of C,H,O with formula (CH2O)n, n=3-7; named by carbonyl type (aldose vs ketose) + carbon count (triose->heptose); combined naming e.g. aldohexose",
    "3. Stereochemistry: chiral carbon = 4 unique groups; enantiomers = non-superimposable mirror images (optically active); diastereomers = multiple chiral centers, not mirror images; meso compound = internal plane of symmetry, optically inactive; racemic mixture = 50/50 enantiomers, optically inactive",
    "4. D/L System: Fischer projection with most oxidized carbon on top; look at OH on the LAST (lowest-numbered from bottom) chiral carbon - right=D, left=L; optical activity measured by polarimeter, D=clockwise(dextrorotatory), L=counterclockwise(levorotatory)",
    "5. Cyclic Structures: physiological ring form via hemiacetal formation (C5-OH attacks carbonyl carbon); creates anomeric carbon with alpha(OH below ring)/beta(OH above ring) forms; glucose=6-membered ring/anomeric C1; fructose=5-membered ring/anomeric C2 (ketose)",
    "6. Key Monosaccharides: Glucose(aldohexose, blood sugar, insulin/glucagon regulated); Fructose(ketohexose, sweetest, fruit sugar); Galactose(aldohexose, differs from glucose only at C4-OH, milk component); Ribose/Deoxyribose(aldopentoses, differ only at C2-OH, RNA vs DNA)",
    "7. Reducing Sugars: require free hemiacetal anomeric OH; Benedict's reagent reduces Cu2+(blue) to Cu+ as Cu2O(red-orange ppt), tests urine glucose; all monosaccharides + all disaccharides except sucrose are reducing; ketoses isomerize to aldoses via enediol intermediate",
    "8. Disaccharides: glycosidic bond forms via condensation (loses H2O); Maltose=glucose+glucose alpha-1,4 (reducing); Lactose=galactose+glucose beta-1,4 (reducing, milk sugar, lactose intolerance from lactase deficiency); Sucrose=alpha-glucose+beta-fructose alpha-1,beta-2 (non-reducing because both anomeric carbons are used in the bond, table sugar, not synthesized by animals); Galactosemia=genetic inability to convert galactose to phosphorylated glucose",
    "9. Polysaccharides: homopolysaccharide(one monomer type) vs heteropolysaccharide(multiple types); Starch in plants: Amylose(alpha-1,4 only, linear/helical, ~80% of starch) and Amylopectin(alpha-1,4 plus alpha-1,6 branches); Glycogen(animal storage, liver/muscle, more frequently branched than amylopectin); Cellulose(plant fiber, beta-1,4 linkages, indigestible by human enzymes, digestible by bacteria in ruminants like cows); Key distinction: starch(digestible) vs cellulose/fiber(indigestible)"
